{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Today', title: "A day's cosmos<br>in one scroll",
  shots: [ { src: shots + '01_today_sun.webp', alt: 'Today tab — SDO live Sun + Today\'s Space + four tabs', w: 720, h: 1454 } ] }) %}
  <p>Open the app and the first thing you see is The Sun Now — the latest frame NASA SDO just took, with its metadata. Scroll down and the day's cosmos continues.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Today's Space (APOD)</strong> — NASA's picture of the day, curated and explained</li>
    <li><strong>Hubble · JWST · ESO Latest</strong>, and <strong>Mars surface</strong> from Curiosity and Perseverance</li>
    <li>DSCOVR EPIC's <strong>Earth</strong>, and <strong>Today's Moon</strong> with phase, distance and the next full Moon</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Sun', title: 'A star<br>in 19 channels', soft: true, flip: true,
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '05_sun_channels.webp', alt: 'Sun fullscreen — AIA 171 Å, 24h timelapse playback', w: 720, h: 1454 },
    { src: shots + '06_earth.webp', alt: 'Sun channel picker — AIA / HMI / Composite groups', w: 720, h: 1454 }
  ] }) %}
  <p>SDO publishes 19 Sun channels, but nothing tells you which channel shows what. The picker sorts them into three groups and labels each one (e.g. "AIA 171 Å · 0.6 MK · quiet corona · coronal loops").</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>A <strong>24-hour timelapse</strong> — watch flares and coronal loops actually move</li>
    <li><strong>AIA 9 · HMI 6 · Composite 4</strong> — coronal layers, photosphere and magnetic field, RGB composites</li>
    <li>A <strong>1024 px high-res</strong> option and a cellular auto-download toggle</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Moon', title: 'A Moon you can<br>turn in your hand',
  shots: [ { src: shots + '08_saturn.webp', alt: 'Moon 3D viewer — phase / illumination / age / distance + phase simulation', w: 720, h: 1454 } ] }) %}
  <p>The Moon isn't a photo — it's an interactive 3D model, built on terrain measured by NASA LRO·LROC·LOLA and textured with SVS CGI Moon Kit. A state panel sums up the day in one line.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>20 landmark pins</strong> — 6 Apollo landings · 5 craters · 5 maria · 4 modern uncrewed missions</li>
    <li><strong>Full light · Lat/Lon grid · Auto rotate</strong> toggles</li>
    <li>A <strong>phase simulation</strong> that auto-plays the change toward Full Moon</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Explore', title: '30+ Solar System bodies,<br>in 3D', soft: true, flip: true,
  shots: [
    { src: shots + '04_sun_timelapse.webp', alt: 'Solar System Explore — inner / outer concentric maps with a time slider', w: 720, h: 1454 },
    { src: shots + '09_pluto.webp', alt: 'Saturn 3D body viewer — magnitude, distance, apparent diameter, rise/set', w: 720, h: 1454 }
  ] }) %}
  <p>The whole Solar System as two concentric maps, inner and outer planets. Drag the time slider and the planets move along real-scale orbits — from where they are today to 100 days from now.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li>The Sun, eight planets with their major moons, and Pluto (Charon) — <strong>30+ bodies</strong></li>
    <li>A <strong>3D model viewer</strong> for each — magnitude, distance, apparent size, today's rise and set</li>
    <li>Discovery year and discoverer, key missions, and <strong>a short description</strong></li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.feat({ eyebrow: 'Sky', title: "What's visible<br>where you are tonight",
  shots: [ { src: shots + '03_explore.webp', alt: "Tonight's Sky — sun rise/set, Moon, next eclipses, planet rise/set", w: 720, h: 1454 } ] }) %}
  <p>Tonight's celestial events at your location. Location is estimated from your IP — no GPS permission — and astronomy-engine computes the values for where you actually are.</p>
  <ul class="checks">
    <li><strong>Sunrise, sunset and twilight</strong>; the Moon's phase, moonrise and moonset</li>
    <li>The next <strong>eclipse</strong> with "Visible here / Not visible here", the next <strong>meteor shower</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Aurora</strong> visibility from NOAA Kp, and <strong>planet</strong> rise/set from Mercury to Neptune</li>
  </ul>
{% endcall %}

{% call m.more('See all features', 'Earth · Gallery · Sun channels · Moon landmarks · Bodies · Settings') %}
  <div>
    <h4>Earth · Gallery</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Earth — DSCOVR EPIC at Lagrange L1 (about 1.5 million km), the full day-side disk, 48-hour timelapse</li>
      <li>Night mode — Black Marble 2016 · Suomi NPP VIIRS composite: city lights, fishing fleets, fires, gas flares</li>
      <li>Gallery tab — Hubble Latest · JWST Latest · ESO Latest (ground telescopes / Chile)</li>
      <li>Tap a card for fullscreen, with a "View original" button to the source page</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>19 Sun channels</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>AIA (UV) 9 — 94 Å (6 MK · flares), 131 Å, 171 Å (quiet corona), 193 Å (coronal holes), 211 Å (active regions), 304 Å (chromosphere + transition), 335 Å, 1600 Å, 1700 Å (photosphere)</li>
      <li>HMI (visible) 6 — magnetogram, color magnetogram, continuum (sunspots), color continuum, flat continuum, Dopplergram</li>
      <li>Composite 4 — 211·193·171, 304·211·171, 94·335·193, HMI magnetogram + AIA 171</li>
      <li>High-res 1024 px uses about 576 MB of memory (3 GB+ RAM recommended), about 10 MB per channel</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>20 Moon landmarks</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Apollo crewed landings 6 — 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 17</li>
      <li>Craters 5 — Tycho, Copernicus, Plato, Aristarchus, Clavius</li>
      <li>Maria 5 — Imbrium, Serenitatis, Tranquillitatis, Crisium, Procellarum</li>
      <li>Modern uncrewed missions 4 — Chang'e 4 (first far-side landing), Chang'e 5 (sample return), Chandrayaan-3 (first south-polar landing), SLIM (pinpoint landing)</li>
      <li>State example: "New Moon · 4% lit / Age 30.8 d · 358,572 km from Earth · Next full in 13 d"</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div>
    <h4>Bodies, sky details, settings</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Planets and moons — Mercury, Venus, Earth (Moon), Mars (Phobos, Deimos), Jupiter (Io, Europa, Ganymede, Callisto), Saturn (Mimas, Enceladus, Tethys, Dione, Rhea, Titan, Iapetus), Uranus (Miranda, Ariel, Umbriel, Titania), Neptune (Triton)</li>
      <li>Distant, dim bodies like Pluto (mag +14.5, 35.128 AU) use the same 3D viewer</li>
      <li>Eclipse kinds (penumbral / partial / total / annular / hybrid), meteor-shower peak and ZHR, four-level aurora verdict</li>
      <li>On Android 12+, an opt-in Material You accent (default is brand indigo)</li>
      <li>Enjoy it just by scrolling; go deeper and every channel, landmark and texture is labeled with its source</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
{% endcall %}

